## Why This Is Asked

Tests multi-state DP design to enforce a direction-run constraint with obstacles, requiring careful state construction and both memoized and tabular implementations.

## Key Concepts

- DP with extra dimensions for last direction and run-length
- Top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and handling blocked cells; boundary cases when L is large or grid small

## Code Example

```javascript
function countPaths(grid, L){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length, MOD = 1000000007;
  if(grid[0][0] || grid[n-1][m-1]) return 0;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:m},()=>Array.from({length:2},()=>Array(L+1).fill(-1))));
  const endI=n-1, endJ=m-1;
  function dfs(i,j,dir,run){
    if(i===endI && j===endJ) return 1;
    const val = memo[i][j][dir][run];
    if(val!==-1) return val;
    let res = 0;
    // right
    if(j+1<m){
      if((dir===0 && run<L) || dir===1){
        res = (res + dfs(i, j+1, 0, dir===0 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    // down
    if(i+1<n){
      if((dir===1 && run<L) || dir===0){
        res = (res + dfs(i+1, j, 1, dir===1 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    memo[i][j][dir][run] = res;
    return res;
  }
  let ans = (dfs(0,0,0,1) + dfs(0,0,1,1)) % MOD;
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(n m)?
- What changes if you allow a diagonal move on occasion?

## Why This Question Matters

This problem evaluates a candidate's ability to extend standard grid DP problems with additional state dimensions. Modeling exact-turn constraints is essential in path-planning scenarios such as robot navigation, vehicle routing, and game movement where turn penalties or restrictions exist.

## Core Concepts

- **State Design**: Extending traditional grid DP with turn count and last direction dimensions
- **Transition Logic**: Increment turn count when direction changes between moves
- **Space Optimization**: Using row-wise or 2-layer rolling arrays to reduce memory from O(n*m*K) to O(m*K)
- **Edge Cases**: Handling obstacles, impossible turn requirements, and boundary conditions

## Reference Implementation

```javascript
function maxPathSumWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [INF, INF])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === -1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (j + 1 < m && grid[i][j+1] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (i > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0] = Math.max(
              dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0],
              dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i][j+1]
            );
          }
          dp[i][j+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j+1][t][0], dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i][j+1]);
        }
        if (i + 1 < n && grid[i+1][j] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (j > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1] = Math.max(
              dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1],
              dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i+1][j]
            );
          }
          dp[i+1][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t][1], dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i+1][j]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1].flat());
  return result === INF ? -1 : result;
}
```

